<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Dec 3, 2000 · 14:20 UTC</div><h3>Parts, partners and gifts</h3><p>The first real catalog, at the height of the company’s profits. The left column lists the parts business; the partner logos say where management was looking: computers, satellite TV and phones.</p><div class="warn">Note: Frames and some images are stitched from nearby captures.</div><div class="ex">Categories: Batteries &amp; Adapters · Electronic Components · Electronic Kits, Games &amp; Toys · Test &amp; Measurement · Wire, Cable, Hardware &amp; Tools. Partners: MICROSOFT® · RCA™ · COMPAQ™ · SPRINT™ · FLUKE™.</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20001203142000/http://radioshack.com:80/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="2" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www.radioshack.com/</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20050301085318if_/http://www.radioshack.com/" title="Archived www.radioshack.com, captured Mar 1, 2005 · 08:53 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Mar 1, 2005 · 08:53 UTC</div><h3>The phone store</h3><p>The year sales peaked at $5.08B. The home page leads with “Now you can buy wireless online!” and a carrier-plan picker. Parts and accessories sit in the left-hand category list.</p><div class="ex">“Now you can buy wireless online! Choose a phone, choose a plan and activate online.” “Free shipping on orders over $50.”</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20050301085318/http://www.radioshack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="3" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www.radioshack.com/</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20141104182216if_/http://www.radioshack.com/" title="Archived www.radioshack.com, captured Nov 4, 2014 · 18:22 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Nov 4, 2014 · 18:22 UTC</div><h3>The last redesign</h3><p>Three months before Chapter 11. A modern, photo-led site built around “projects” and “services,” the company’s pitch to makers and to people who needed help with their phones.</p><div class="warn">Note: The live page relied on scripts, which the archive view disables, so carousels and menus may not render.</div><div class="ex">Navigation: Shop · Services · Projects · Deals. Hero: “Turn up the tailgate.”</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20141104182216/http://www.radioshack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="4" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">http://www.radioshack.com/</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20170701003943if_/http://www.radioshack.com/" title="Archived www.radioshack.com, captured Jul 1, 2017 · 00:39 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Jul 1, 2017 · 00:39 UTC</div><h3>Selling the furniture</h3><p>After General Wireless, the buyer of the 2015 stores, filed its own Chapter 11 in March 2017. The site sells off stock and memorabilia.</p><div class="ex">“Memorabilia online auction through July 3rd.” “Clearance sale up to 60% off.”</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20170701003943/http://www.radioshack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="5" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">https://www.radioshack.com/</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20220101093831if_/https://www.radioshack.com/" title="Archived www.radioshack.com, captured Jan 1, 2022 · 09:38 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Jan 1, 2022 · 09:38 UTC</div><h3>A crypto start-up</h3><p>Under Retail Ecommerce Ventures the home page became a waitlist for a crypto project, with a red banner warning about impostor accounts.</p><div class="ex">“Bringing Cryptocurrency To The Mainstream.” “Get notified on the upcoming RadioShack launch.” Links: Docs · Discord · Store.</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20220101093831/https://www.radioshack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div><div class="wb-view" data-i="6" hidden>
<figure class="browser"><div class="chrome"><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="cd"></span><span class="url">https://www.radioshack.com/</span></div><div class="shot live"><div class="ld">Loading the archived page from the Internet Archive…</div><iframe data-src="https://web.archive.org/web/20250401012431if_/https://www.radioshack.com/" title="Archived www.radioshack.com, captured Apr 1, 2025 · 01:24 UTC" sandbox="allow-same-origin" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe></div></figure>
<figcaption class="wb-cap"><div class="d">Captured Apr 1, 2025 · 01:24 UTC</div><h3>An online store again</h3><p>Under Unicomer, a conventional e-commerce site selling headphones, speakers, batteries and chargers.</p><div class="warn">Note: The archive view disables scripts, so sliders may appear as stacked images.</div><div class="ex">Promotions: “Shack Spring.” Categories: Headphones · Speakers · Backpacks · Travel · Portable Chargers · Batteries · Home &amp; Office.</div><div class="arch"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20250401012431/https://www.radioshack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open this capture on the Wayback Machine ↗</a></div></figcaption>
</div></div>
<p class="note rv">A March 2000 capture records only a redirect and is excluded. Captures were located with the Wayback CDX API in October 2026. Quoted text was read from the captures.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="economics" class="alt">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">04</span>Store economics</div><h2 class="rv">Same stores, less in each one</h2><p class="lede rv">RadioShack’s model was thousands of small, cheap stores close to customers. It works while each store sells enough high-margin goods. After 2005 sales per store slid, and the growth that remained came from phones, the lowest-margin thing it sold.</p></div>
<div class="card rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>Sales per company store, 1999–2013 (derived)</h3><p>Total net sales and operating revenues divided by company-operated U.S. stores at year end, $ millions. Overstates true store sales, because it includes dealer, online, kiosk and international revenue.</p></div></div>
<div class="cbox"><canvas id="perStoreChart"></canvas></div>
<div class="src">Our calculation from the 10-K sales and store counts<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup>. 2009–2013 use sales as restated in the 2013 10-K.</div>
</div>
<div class="g2">
<div class="card rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>Wireless share of sales, and gross margin</h3><p>Percent. Wireless and “mobility” are the company’s own categories, which it redefined over time, so the lines have gaps.</p></div></div>
<div class="cbox"><canvas id="mixChart"></canvas></div>
<div class="src">Wireless share: 2005 and 2009 10-Ks<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-11">11</a></sup>; mobility share: 2013 10-K<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup>. Gross margin 2002–2005 as reported<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-8">8</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup>; 2011–2013 derived from gross profit and sales in the 2013 10-K.</div>
</div>
<div class="card rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>The postpaid phone squeeze, 2012 and 2013</h3><p>Change from the prior year at U.S. company stores, percent.</p></div></div>
<div class="cbox"><canvas id="postpaidChart"></canvas></div>
<div class="src">2013 10-K, “Update on Postpaid Wireless Business”<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup>. The company sold fewer phones, paid more for each one, and repaid commissions when customers stopped paying for service.</div>
</div>
</div>
<table class="kf compact rv" style="margin-top:44px"><caption>Table 3. The margin problem in four lines</caption><tbody>
<tr><td class="kv">50.3%</td><td>gross margin in 2004, as reported<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-8">8</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td class="kv">34.1%</td><td>gross margin in 2013, derived from $1,172.2M gross profit on $3,434.3M sales<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td class="kv">−23%</td><td>postpaid phones sold at U.S. company stores in 2013, after −20% in 2012<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td class="kv">1,522</td><td>Target Mobile centers it ran inside Target stores at the end of 2012. It quit all of them by March 31, 2013<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p class="note rv">RadioShack didn’t report profit by store, so we can’t say how many stores lost money. The 2013 10-K doesn’t give a gross margin rate directly, which is why we derive it.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section id="marketing">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">05</span>Marketing</div><h2 class="rv">Three ways of saying “we’re not your dad’s RadioShack”</h2><p class="lede rv">For most of its life the pitch was service: “You’ve got questions. We’ve got answers.” Later campaigns tried to escape the image of a dusty parts store, and spent less money each time.</p></div>
<div class="tbl-scroll rv"><table class="cmp">
<tr><th>Campaign</th><th>Years</th><th>What it said</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
<tr><td>“You’ve got questions. We’ve got answers.”</td><td>Tandy era onward</td><td>The store clerk as expert. The trademark was still among the assets sold in 2020<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-28">28</a></sup></td><td>Became the brand’s best-remembered line</td></tr>
<tr><td>Partners: Microsoft, Compaq, RCA, Sprint</td><td>c. 1999–2001</td><td>Store-within-a-store alliances shown on the home page<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-33">33</a></sup></td><td>Wireless kept growing, computers did not (no sales split in our sources)</td></tr>
<tr><td>“The Shack”</td><td>Aug 2009</td><td>An advertising nickname to modernize the image. Signs and the legal name stayed RadioShack<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-23">23</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></td><td>Advertising spend fell to $193.0M that year<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-11">11</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>“The ’80s called. They want their store back.”</td><td>Feb 2014</td><td>Super Bowl XLVIII ad in which 1980s celebrities empty an old-style store<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-24">24</a></sup></td><td>Chapter 11 followed a year later<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-15">15</a></sup></td></tr>
</table></div>
<div class="card rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>Advertising expense, 1999–2013</h3><p>$ millions (bars) and as a share of net sales (line, derived). As first reported in each 10-K.</p></div></div>
<div class="cbox"><canvas id="adChart"></canvas></div>
<div class="src">Advertising expense from the 10-K notes for each year<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-5">5</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-8">8</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-11">11</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup>. 2001 and 2003 are computed from the year-on-year changes the filings state.</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="money" class="alt">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">06</span>The money</div><h2 class="rv">A $79 stock that fell below $3</h2><p class="lede rv">RadioShack’s shares more than tripled in 1999. They spent the next decade in the $15–$35 range, then fell below $3 in 2012, as investors priced in what the 10-Ks were starting to show.</p></div>
<div class="card rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>RSH share price, quarterly high and low, 1996–2013</h3><p>NYSE, $ per share, adjusted for the 1999 two-for-one split. Hover for the quarter.</p></div></div>
<div class="cbox tall"><canvas id="stockChart"></canvas></div>
<div class="src">Quarterly price ranges from the market-information section of each 10-K<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-11">11</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup>. 1996–1997 values are halved by us for the split. We don’t have a sourced 2014 range.</div>
</div>
<div class="g2">
<div class="card rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>Cash, long-term debt and equity, 2009–2014</h3><p>$ millions at year end. 2014 is November 1 cash only.</p></div></div>
<div class="cbox"><canvas id="balChart"></canvas></div>
<div class="src">XBRL financial data from the 10-K and 10-Q filings<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-12">12</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-14">14</a></sup>, as reported in the most recent filing for each date.</div>
</div>
<div class="card rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>Gross profit against operating costs, by quarter</h3><p>$ millions, 2012–2013. When the gap closes, the stores stop paying for themselves.</p></div></div>
<div class="cbox"><canvas id="gpChart"></canvas></div>
<div class="src">Quarterly XBRL data filed with the 2013 10-K and 2014 10-Qs<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-14">14</a></sup>. Operating costs are selling, general and administrative expense, before depreciation and impairments.</div>
</div>
</div>
<table class="kf compact rv" style="margin-top:44px"><caption>Table 4. The bankruptcy balance sheet</caption><tbody>
<tr><td class="kv">$1.2B</td><td>assets listed in the February 2015 Chapter 11 filing<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-25">25</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td class="kv">$1.38B</td><td>debts listed in the same filing<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-25">25</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td class="kv">$31.9M</td><td>paid by Office Depot de México for the Mexican subsidiaries in 2015<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-16">16</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td class="kv">15,969</td><td>holders of record on Feb 21, 2014, down from 31,087 on Mar 21, 2001<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-5">5</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td class="kv">$12M</td><td>paid by Sprint under a settlement in the second, General Wireless bankruptcy<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-27">27</a></sup></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
</section>

<section id="map">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">07</span>The map</div><h2 class="rv">Everywhere, and too many</h2><p class="lede rv">At the end of 2013, a year before bankruptcy, RadioShack still operated 4,297 company stores in every state but Alaska, plus 901 dealer outlets that reached the smallest towns. Two years of losses had closed only 179 company stores since 2009.</p></div>
<figure class="map-fig rv">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3 data-n="1">RadioShack stores by state, 2013</h3><p><b>What this map shows:</b> company-operated stores in each state at December 31, 2013, shaded by count. Switch views to see dealer outlets or the change since 2009. Hover over a state for numbers. The two pins mark where the company began and where it was run.</p></div><div class="seg" id="mapSeg"><button class="on" data-v="stores">Company stores</button><button data-v="dealers">Dealer outlets</button><button data-v="change">Change since 2009</button></div></div>
<div class="choro-legend" id="choroLeg"></div>
<div class="mapbox" style="margin-top:12px;aspect-ratio:975/610;position:relative"><svg class="ov" id="mapRS" viewBox="0 0 975 610" role="img" aria-label="Map of the United States shaded by number of RadioShack stores in each state"></svg><div class="maptip"></div></div>
<div class="notes-m" id="notesRS"></div>
<ol class="map-key" id="keyRS"></ol>
<figcaption>Store counts by state are from the store tables in the 2013 and 2009 10-Ks<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-11">11</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup>. Puerto Rico (58 stores) and the U.S. Virgin Islands (4) are not drawn. Base map: U.S. Census Bureau via us-atlas<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-34">34</a></sup>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="card rv" style="margin-top:48px">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>U.S. company stores and dealer outlets, 1995–2014</h3><p>At year end. Dealer counts stop after 2004 in our extracted data. 2014 is November 1.</p></div></div>
<div class="cbox tall"><canvas id="countChart"></canvas></div>
